WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.989
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:10.081
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.381 --> 00:00:17.829
Unit eighteen: Social Welfare Systems. Today you will concede points with however, whatever and no matter how.

4
00:00:18.129 --> 00:00:20.653
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.914
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.308
Social safety net.

7
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:33.416
Social safety net means government support that protects people from falling into poverty.

8
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.351
A social safety net helps people who lose their jobs.

9
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.881
Your turn. Say: Social safety net.

10
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:45.350
Social safety net.

11
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:47.735
Unemployment benefit.

12
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.816
Unemployment benefit means money paid to people who have lost their jobs.

13
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.264
Unemployment benefit lasted for six months.

14
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.793
Your turn. Say: Unemployment benefit.

15
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:04.215
Unemployment benefit.

16
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.334
Public assistance.

17
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.836
Public assistance means government help for people with low income.

18
00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.541
Public assistance covers food and housing costs.

19
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.081
Your turn. Say: Public assistance.

20
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:22.434
Public assistance.

21
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.440
Welfare reform.

22
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.098
Welfare reform means changes to the rules of the benefit system.

23
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:32.716
Welfare reform aimed to help people return to work.

24
00:01:33.317 --> 00:01:36.677
Your turn. Say: Welfare reform.

25
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:40.952
Welfare reform.

26
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:43.182
Poverty reduction.

27
00:01:44.183 --> 00:01:49.062
Poverty reduction means actions that lower the number of people living in poverty.

28
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.822
Poverty reduction requires jobs as well as benefits.

29
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.441
Your turn. Say: Poverty reduction.

30
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.626
Poverty reduction.

31
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:03.209
Universal basic income.

32
00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:08.318
Universal basic income means a regular payment made to every citizen.

33
00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:12.367
Universal basic income was tested in several cities.

34
00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:16.344
Your turn. Say: Universal basic income.

35
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.072
Universal basic income.

36
00:02:21.817 --> 00:02:23.096
Means testing.

37
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:27.975
Means testing means checking a person's income before giving benefits.

38
00:02:28.583 --> 00:02:31.338
Means testing ensures help reaches those in need.

39
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.579
Your turn. Say: Means testing.

40
00:02:37.433 --> 00:02:38.760
Means testing.

41
00:02:39.517 --> 00:02:40.901
Social security.

42
00:02:41.917 --> 00:02:46.563
Social security means a state system that pays money to people in need or retired.

43
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.705
Social security provides a pension for older citizens.

44
00:02:51.317 --> 00:02:54.317
Your turn. Say: Social security.

45
00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:58.621
Social security.

46
00:02:59.433 --> 00:03:02.864
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:10.897
Visal, a columnist writes 'However generous the benefit is, some families still fall into poverty.' What does 'however generous' mean?

48
00:03:11.983 --> 00:03:25.958
It means 'no matter how generous'. Structure: however + adjective/adverb + subject + verb. Similar forms are whatever, whoever, wherever and whichever, followed by a clause.

49
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.783
Is the word order special?

50
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:43.359
Yes: the adjective comes directly after however, before the subject. We say 'However poor a family is', not 'However a family is poor'. The clause usually comes first, and a comma follows.

51
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.159
Show me a welfare example.

52
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:59.643
Whatever form welfare reform takes, a social safety net must protect the poorest. However costly universal basic income seems, supporters say poverty reduction saves money.

53
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.637
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:03.483 --> 00:04:07.735
Grammar focus: Concession: however plus adjective and whatever.

55
00:04:08.035 --> 00:04:09.222
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:09.917 --> 00:04:14.378
However generous the benefit is, some families still fall into poverty.

57
00:04:15.283 --> 00:04:19.442
Whatever form welfare reform takes, a safety net must remain.

58
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.836
No matter how costly the scheme seems, supporters defend it.

59
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:34.363
However / no matter how + adjective + subject + verb. Whatever / whoever / wherever + clause. Keep the adjective right after however and put a comma after the clause.

60
00:04:35.117 --> 00:04:39.499
Be careful! Don't say: However a family is poor, it needs help.

61
00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:44.129
Say: However poor a family is, it needs help.

62
00:04:44.429 --> 00:04:46.487
Put the adjective right after 'however'.

63
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:50.324
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:52.889
Safety Nets Under Debate.

65
00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:57.348
A social safety net exists to catch people when life becomes difficult.

66
00:04:58.333 --> 00:05:04.919
However well designed a system is, it will cost money, and however careful officials are, some people will be missed.

67
00:05:05.933 --> 00:05:11.892
Unemployment benefit and public assistance help families through a crisis, whatever its cause.

68
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:21.331
Supporters of means testing say it focuses help on those in need, whereas supporters of universal basic income prefer simplicity.

69
00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:27.061
No matter how welfare reform is organised, poverty reduction should remain the main goal.

70
00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:32.308
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:36.148
blank generous the benefit is, some families struggle.

72
00:05:36.448 --> 00:05:40.194
A, However. B, Whatever. C, Whoever.

73
00:05:43.733 --> 00:05:45.617
The answer is A: However.

74
00:05:46.783 --> 00:05:49.317
Checking income before giving benefits is blank.

75
00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:54.791
A, social security. B, means testing. C, public assistance.

76
00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:00.832
The answer is B: means testing.

77
00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:03.727
Choose the correct sentence.

78
00:06:04.027 --> 00:06:12.348
A, However a family is poor, it needs help.. B, However poor is a family, it needs help.. C, However poor a family is, it needs help..

79
00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.862
The answer is C: However poor a family is, it needs help..

80
00:06:21.017 --> 00:06:24.108
blank form the reform takes, the safety net remains.

81
00:06:24.408 --> 00:06:27.748
A, Whatever. B, However. C, Wherever.

82
00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:32.940
The answer is A: Whatever.

83
00:06:34.083 --> 00:06:38.591
According to the reading, the main goal of welfare reform should be poverty blank.

84
00:06:38.891 --> 00:06:42.788
A, reduction. B, tests. C, taxes.

85
00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:48.275
The answer is A: reduction.

86
00:06:49.467 --> 00:06:52.467
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:53.317 --> 00:06:58.521
Should welfare programs use means testing, or should benefits be universal for everyone?

88
00:07:07.317 --> 00:07:08.817
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:09.117 --> 00:07:35.889
Means testing directs limited money to the people who need it most, which can make a system cheaper and fairer. However, it can be complicated, stigmatising and may leave out families just above the line. Universal benefits are simpler and avoid shame, but they cost more. A good compromise is a universal basic level with extra support for those in greatest need.

90
00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:37.886
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:38.783 --> 00:07:44.721
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and concession: however plus adjective and whatever.

92
00:07:45.021 --> 00:07:50.297
Today's value is social justice. Work for a world where everyone is treated fairly.

93
00:07:50.597 --> 00:07:53.436
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:53.736 --> 00:07:56.585
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

